# SDD Best Practices

Spec: SDDBestPractices
Purpose:
  Curated best practices for Spec-Driven Development, synthesized
  from industry research (Allegro Tech Blog, Medium, and practitioner
  guides). These are reference-level guidance, not project rules.

Owns: references/sdd/best-practices.sdd

ThreeLevelsOfSDD:
  Spec-First:
    description: well thought-out spec written first, then used in AI workflow
  Spec-Anchored:
    description: spec kept alongside code, both evolve together, both reviewed
  Spec-as-Source:
    description: spec is the main source file; code is regenerable artifact

  focus: Spec-Anchored (specification lives alongside code, evolves together)

CoreWorkflow:
  Specify → Plan → Tasks → Implement

  Specify:
    - gather product/functional requirements
    - gather technical/non-functional requirements
    - gather integration requirements (API contracts, events)
    - prepare mockups
    - write acceptance criteria in EARS notation

  Plan:
    - translate spec into technical plan
    - architecture choices and rationale
    - data model and schemas
    - API contracts
    - library/framework selections (from project constitution)
    - migration strategy

  Tasks:
    - decompose plan into atomic, independently-shippable tasks
    - each task has: single objective, inputs, outputs, acceptance check

  Implement:
    - delegate tasks to coding agents
    - verify against acceptance criteria

SpecMustContains:
  MUST:
    - product description (what, why)
    - users and roles
    - functional requirements (features)
    - non-functional requirements (reference shared doc)
    - UI description
    - use cases (especially complex logic and edge cases)

  SHOULD:
    - technical specification (architecture, ADRs, stack)
    - data model
    - API definition
    - security and authentication
    - edge cases, non-happy paths

  SHOULD_NOT:
    - implementation details (unless necessary)
    - written by one person only

RiskManagement:
  LowCriticality = low consequences = low control → greater acceleration
  HighCriticality = high consequences = high control → less acceleration

  principle: specification detail level must depend on risk level

SeparationWhatFromHow:
  Specification: what to build (blueprint)
  ExecutionPlan: which piece to build next, how (volatile, can be discarded)

  rule: keep specification and execution plan as separate documents

SharedTechnicalRequirements:
  Shared file (e.g., SHARED-TECH-SPEC.md) defines org-wide standards:
    - Availability & Reliability
    - Performance & Scalability
    - Security & Privacy
    - Organizational Guidelines

  principle: individual specs reference shared requirements, not repeat them

SpecificationGranularity:
  FeaturePerSpec:
    works_better_for: medium-to-large projects, brownfield
    benefits: lighter context window, easier maintenance, survives over time
  MonolithicSpec:
    works_better_for: small projects, greenfield
    drawbacks: context size problems, hard to maintain as project grows

QualityGates:
  Pre-implementation: review the spec
  Mid-implementation: run tests against acceptance criteria
  Post-implementation: cleanup agent scan for scout-rule improvements
  Rule: do not skip phase boundaries (spec → plan → tasks → code)

HumanReview:
  principle: humans review at every phase boundary
  spec reviewed before plan
  plan reviewed before tasks
  tasks reviewed before implementation
  Rule: "you are accountable for the code in production, regardless of who wrote it"

EARSNotation:
  Ubiquitous: "The system shall log every authentication attempt."
  Event-driven: "WHEN [trigger] THE [system] SHALL [response]"
  State-driven: "WHILE [state] THE [system] SHALL [behavior]"
  Unwanted: "IF [condition] THEN THE [system] SHALL [response]"
  Optional: "WHERE [feature is included] THE [system] SHALL [behavior]"

  principle: each pattern collapses to a single testable claim

State: +
